<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samnite Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/343-bce">343</a>–290 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samnite Wars were three wars fought between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the Samnites of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> between 343 and 290 BCE. Despite defeats such as the Caudine Forks in 321 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> prevailed and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sentinum">Battle of Sentinum</a> in 295 BCE, became the dominant power in central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Neighbouring peoples who sided with the Samnites, such as the Aurunci, were crushed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samnite-wars.jpg" alt="Samnite Wars" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Javierfv1212 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_conquest_of_Italy.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Samnites were a confederation of four tribes living in the mountains east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. Rome and the Samnites made a treaty in 354 BCE whose terms are not recorded; modern historians have proposed that it made the River Liris the boundary between their spheres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the only continuous source for the first war, says it began when the Samnites attacked the Sidicini and then the Campanians, and Capua surrendered itself to Rome. Modern historians doubt his three Roman victories of 343 BCE. The war ended in 341 BCE in a negotiated peace: Rome accepted that the Sidicini lay in the Samnite sphere but kept its alliance with the Campanians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The second war grew out of Roman moves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>: a colony founded at Fregellae in 328 BCE and the taking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 326 BCE. In 321 BCE the consuls Titus Veturius Calvinus and Spurius Postumius Albinus were trapped by the Samnite commander Gaius Pontius in the Caudine Forks and surrendered; their soldiers passed unarmed under the yoke and six hundred equites were given as hostages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> rejected the agreement and avenged it, a claim some modern scholars doubt. Fighting resumed in 316 BCE, and in 304 BCE, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bovianum">Bovianum</a>, the Samnites sought peace; Livy says the old treaty was restored.</p><p>The third war began in 298 BCE, when the Lucanians asked for Roman protection against Samnite invasion. In 295 BCE Rome faced a coalition of Samnites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, Umbrians and Senone Gauls. At Sentinum the consul Publius Decius Mus devoted himself, charging into the enemy line to his death, and Quintus Fabius Maximus Rullianus won the battle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> gives 8,700 Roman dead and 20,000 of the enemy. Livy's surviving narrative ends in 293 BCE, and the last campaigns to 290 BCE are known only in outline.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Samnites were forced to become allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, on terms that must have been unequal, and a colony was planted at Venusia in south-eastern Samnia. Gaius Pontius was captured, paraded in a triumph and beheaded. Around Samnia Rome had already bound the Marsi, Marrucini, Paeligni and Frentani by alliance in 304 BCE and the Vestini in 302 BCE, and it annexed the Hernici and the Sabines by giving them citizenship without the vote. The allies supplied soldiers at their own expense and shared the spoils, which greatly enlarged the manpower Rome could call on.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samnite Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After they joined Rome's enemies during the <b>Samnite Wars</b>, Rome destroyed the towns of Ausona, Minturnae and Vescia in 314 BCE, massacring much of the population.</blockquote><a href="/state/aurunci">Aurunci</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Samnite Wars</b> the Daunian towns sided with Rome; Livy records alliances in 318 BCE, and Rome founded Luceria in 314 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a></li><li><blockquote>As part of Samnium they fought Rome in the <b>Samnite Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hirpini">Hirpini</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome fought three <b>Samnite Wars</b> from 343 to 290 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/samnite-league">Samnite League</a></li></ul></section>
